<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (Q1. Explain the concept of a "lane" in PCIe and how it affects throughput.)
/E (Q1. Explain the concept of a "lane" in PCIe and how it affects throughput.)
/Pg 8 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
138 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (A lane in PCIe consists of two differential pairs: one for transmitting and one for receiving data. PCIe supports 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, and 32 lane configurations. Throughput scales linearly with the number of lanes. For example, a PCIe Gen3 x8 link has 8 lanes, each supporting 8 GT/s \(after 128b/130b encoding, ~7.877 Gbps effective\), giving a total throughput of ~63.0 Gbps per direction.)
/E (A lane in PCIe consists of two differential pairs: one for transmitting and one for receiving data. PCIe supports 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, and 32 lane configurations. Throughput scales linearly with the number of lanes. For example, a PCIe Gen3 x8 link has 8 lanes, each supporting 8 GT/s \(after 128b/130b encoding, ~7.877 Gbps effective\), giving a total throughput of ~63.0 Gbps per direction.)
/Pg 8 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
